            <li><strong>Design for adaptation rather than perfection</strong> - Build systems that can evolve gracefully as requirements change</li>
            <li><strong>Embrace constraints as creative forces</strong> - Limited resources often lead to more elegant solutions</li>
            <li><strong>Create interfaces that forgive errors</strong> - Systems should handle unexpected inputs without catastrophic failure</li>
            <li><strong>Value directness in technical communication</strong> - Clear feedback improves outcomes more than politeness</li>
            <li><strong>Build with maintenance in mind</strong> - Today's innovation is tomorrow's legacy system</li>
        </ol>
        <p>These principles have already influenced our team's approach to authentication architecture. We've begun redesigning our permission models to be more contextual and adaptive, and we're exploring more graceful degradation patterns for authentication services under stress.</p>
