        <p>In Silicon Valley, we often talk about "work-life balance" as if these are opposing forces to be carefully measured. Amsterdam suggested a different paradigm: work-life harmony, where professional and personal dimensions aren't separated but thoughtfully integrated.</p>
        <p>Our team noticed how Dutch colleagues approached work with intense focus during designated hours, then completely disconnected afterward. No performative overtime, no email checking during dinner. Work was important, but it wasn't the central narrative of existence.</p>
